    <body>
      <sec id="article-170151.s1" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>A scrotal abscess is a localized collection of purulent material in the wall of the scrotum, involving the skin and subcutaneous tissues. The scrotum is a pendulous sac composed of skin, dartos muscle, and multiple fascial layers, including the external spermatic cord, cremasteric muscle and fascia, internal spermatic cord, and tunica vaginalis. Each hemiscrotum contains the testis, epididymis, and spermatic cord. The rich vascular supply and loose connective tissue of the scrotum predispose the area to rapid spread of infection.</p>
        <p>Scrotal abscesses typically develop following bacterial inoculation, skin infections, or extension from deeper adjacent infections. Predisposing factors include&#x000a0;diabetes mellitus,&#x000a0;immunosuppression, poor hygiene, tobacco use, advanced age, alcohol use disorder, obesity, renal failure, atherosclerosis, and malnutrition.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;Scrotal abscesses are distinct from other intrascrotal pathologies, which can be differentiated by ultrasonography.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>The natural history of a scrotal abscess is characterized by an acute inflammatory response with pain, swelling, and erythema that develops over several days, culminating in the accumulation of purulent material. Diagnosis is typically based on the patient's history and physical examination, and management typically consists of surgical incision and drainage. Clinicians should be vigilant for potential complications, including sepsis and the life-threatening necrotizing infection known as Fournier gangrene. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://statpearls.com/point-of-care/21939">Fournier Gangrene</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-170151.s2" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Scrotal abscesses typically develop due to a local bacterial infection of the scrotal skin or subcutaneous tissues following minor skin trauma (eg, shaving) or folliculitis. <italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic> is the most common causative organism.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;The local immune&#x000a0;response&#x000a0;in scrotal abscess formation involves significant neutrophil recruitment, leading to enzymatic tissue digestion and the formation of an abscess cavity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>In some cases, scrotal abscesses may develop after a deeper perineal infection, most commonly caused by&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Streptococcus pyogenes</italic>&#x000a0;and anaerobic bacteria.&#x000a0;Rarely,&#x000a0;remote intra-abdominal processes, such as ruptured appendicitis, pancreatitis, or bowel perforation, may lead to scrotal abscess development.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resources, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://statpearls.com/point-of-care/21939">Fournier Gangrene</ext-link>" and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://statpearls.com/point-of-care/42960">Streptococcus Pyogenes</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
        <p>Tuberculous scrotal abscesses are quite rare in the United States; however, globally, they account for 3%&#x000a0;to 7% of all extrapulmonary tuberculosis cases, particularly in regions with higher tuberculosis prevalence, including parts of Africa, China, and India.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r16">[16]</xref></p>
        <p>In children, scrotal abscesses may be associated with&#x000a0;epididymo-orchitis, undiagnosed and untreated testicular torsion, spread of an intra-abdominal infectious process through a patent processus vaginalis, a hematogenously spread infection, ejaculatory duct dysfunction, caudal obstruction of the Wolffian duct, vesicoureteral reflux, or ectopic ureteral insertion into the posterior urethra.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r21">[21]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-170151.s3" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Precise epidemiological data on isolated scrotal abscesses are currently unavailable. These conditions are often managed in the outpatient or emergency settings and often coded under general skin and soft tissue infections; therefore, site-specific data are lacking. Results from a large retrospective cohort study&#x000a0;in the United States reported an incidence of 496 skin and soft tissue infections per 10,000 person-years, with abscesses accounting for a significant proportion of cases. Although this study did not report anatomical or site-specific data, it provides a useful estimate of the burden of abscess-related infections in the general population.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;More recent data, however, suggest that the rise of skin and soft tissue infections in the early 2000s has plateaued and may be declining.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r23">[23]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r24">[24]</xref></p>
        <p>Risk factors for scrotal abscess formation are similar to those for subcutaneous abscesses at other skin sites.&#x000a0;Common risk factors include diabetes mellitus, immunosuppressive states, obesity, poor personal hygiene, chronic kidney disease, smoking, injection drug use, long-term Foley catheter use, history of scrotal trauma or urogenital surgery, urinary tract or perineal infections, epididymo-orchitis, sexually transmitted diseases, HIV, alcohol use disorder, advanced age, and various underlying dermatological conditions, such as hidranenitis suppuriativa.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r26">[26]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r28">[28]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r30">[30]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/22863">Hidradenitis Suppurativa</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
        <p>The use of sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 (SGLT2) inhibitors has recently emerged as a potential risk factor for Fournier gangrene.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r31">[31]</xref> These medications inhibit the sodium glucose cotransporter in the proximal renal tubules.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r32">[32]</xref> This cotransporter uses the intracellular sodium gradient generated by the sodium-potassium adenosine triphosphatase pump to reabsorb glucose from the urine into the bloodstream.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r32">[32]</xref>&#x000a0;Inhibition of SGLT2 reduces glucose reabsorption, leading to significant glucosuria.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r32">[32]</xref> These medications have only rarely been linked to Fournier gangrene, but the United States Food and Drug Administration and the European Medicines Agency have released a box warning regarding this possible association.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r31">[31]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r33">[33]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r34">[34]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r35">[35]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r36">[36]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r37">[37]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r38">[38]</xref>&#x000a0;The association is not definitive because diabetes mellitus is also a risk factor for Fournier gangrene, and findings from recent reviews have not confirmed a causal relationship.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r34">[34]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r38">[38]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r39">[39]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r40">[40]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-170151.s4" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>The skin is the body's first line of defense against microbial invasion, providing both a physical barrier and an immunological shield against invading pathogens. Scrotal abscesses may form when scrotal skin is disrupted, allowing bacteria to enter the subcutaneous tissue. This infection triggers a local acute inflammatory response, involving activation of the innate immune system and the accumulation of neutrophils at the site of infection.&#x000a0;Neutrophils release enzymes and reactive oxygen species to destroy invading organisms, causing liquefactive necrosis and local tissue damage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;This accumulation of neutrophils, bacteria, and cellular debris forms the purulent material within the abscess cavity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r41">[41]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r42">[42]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-170151.s5" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>Microscopically, subcutaneous abscesses show features of acute suppurative inflammation, with a cavity filled with purulent material composed of neutrophils, bacteria, necrotic cellular debris, and exudate. The surrounding subcutaneous tissue may be edematous and demonstrate infiltration with inflammatory cells, predominantly phagocytic cells. In chronic abscesses, granulation tissue and fibroblast proliferation occur, leading to fibrosis and scarring.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;In scrotal wall abscesses, inflammatory changes are typically confined to the subcutaneous tissues and dermis, with no involvement of the epididymis, testis, or other intrascrotal structures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r5">[5]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-170151.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Obtaining a detailed history and performing a thorough physical examination are often sufficient to diagnose a scrotal abscess. Patients typically present with a painful, tender scrotum with localized swelling that develops over several days. Patients may also present with fever or systemic symptoms, suggesting possible sepsis.</p>
        <p>On physical examination, a superficial, palpable mass is identified within the scrotal wall. This mass is usually tender, fluctuant, with surrounding erythema, warmth, or features of cellulitis. If the abscess has ruptured, purulent material may exude from the wound. The underlying testis and epididymis are typically nontender and anatomically normal, distinguishing scrotal abscess from other intrascrotal pathologies.</p>
        <p>Red flags suggesting complications include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Crepitus on palpation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Gas in the abscess cavity on ultrasonography</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Necrotic skin&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Rapid progression of infection</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Severe systemic illness, fever, or sepsis</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>These features may suggest Fournier gangrene, a form of necrotizing fasciitis that affects the superficial and deep soft tissues of the scrotal, perineal, and perianal regions and requires urgent surgical intervention. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://statpearls.com/point-of-care/21939">Fournier Gangrene</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
        <p>Scrotal pyoceles&#x000a0;are rare infectious disorders of the scrotum.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r43">[43]</xref>&#x000a0;These disorders often present as a&#x000a0;collection of purulent fluid in the hydrocele space surrounding the testicle, and are typically associated with a scrotal infection or epididymo-orchitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r43">[43]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r44">[44]</xref>&#x000a0;Clinical symptoms include subacute scrotal pain, edema, and erythema.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r43">[43]</xref></p>
        <p>Scrotal pyoceles can be differentiated from hydroceles by their clinical symptoms and ultrasonographic findings, including air-fluid levels and abnormal internal echoes within the hydrocele sac, which indicate loculations, debris, and septations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r43">[43]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r45">[45]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r46">[46]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r47">[47]</xref>&#x000a0;Treatment involves broad-spectrum antibiotics and surgical drainage; however, many cases ultimately require an orchiectomy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r43">[43]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r44">[44]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r48">[48]</xref>&#x000a0;If left untreated, this condition may progress to Fournier gangrene.&#x000a0;If Fournier gangrene is a consideration, a&#x000a0;computed tomography scan is recommended to better define the extent and margins of the infection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r43">[43]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r49">[49]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see the companion StatPearls'&#x000a0;resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://statpearls.com/point-of-care/21939">Fournier Gangrene</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-170151.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>The diagnosis is primarily clinical; however, it may be supported by imaging (scrotal ultrasonography) and laboratory testing. Routine clinical assessments include oxygen saturation, respiratory rate, heart rate, blood pressure, and temperature. Blood testing is not routinely required; however, if diagnostic uncertainty persists, a complete blood count and C-reactive protein level may be helpful. In patients who are systemically unwell or have features of sepsis, a more comprehensive panel of blood tests should be performed, including renal and liver function tests, a coagulation profile, blood gas analysis, lactate and glucose levels, and blood cultures.</p>
        <p>A hemoglobin A1c level may be considered to screen for undiagnosed diabetes mellitus. Urinalysis may be performed at the bedside to help exclude a urinary tract infection because results are typically normal in an isolated scrotal abscess.</p>
        <p>Scrotal ultrasonography helps distinguish an abscess from other scrotal&#x000a0;masses and testicular pathologies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r50">[50]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r51">[51]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r52">[52]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r53">[53]</xref>&#x000a0;Typical sonographic&#x000a0;features of a scrotal abscess&#x000a0;include&#x000a0;an avascular localized hypoechoic or complex fluid collection with posterior acoustic enhancement within the scrotal wall, often with septations and debris, usually surrounded by soft-tissue edema or cellulitis, producing a cobblestone appearance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r50">[50]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r51">[51]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r52">[52]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r53">[53]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r54">[54]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r55">[55]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r56">[56]</xref></p>
        <p>Doppler vascular flow color imaging demonstrates an avascular area corresponding to the abscess cavity&#x000a0;with a surrounding area of markedly increased vascularity corresponding to adjacent inflammation and cellulitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r50">[50]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r51">[51]</xref>&#x000a0;The presence of gas within the abscess is an ominous sign and is characteristic of Fournier gangrene.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r57">[57]</xref>&#x000a0;Magnetic resonance imaging may also be used; however, ultrasonography is preferred initially due to its low cost, availability, and quickness.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r58">[58]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r59">[59]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-170151.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>
<bold>Antibiotic Selection&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>Incision and drainage remain the definitive treatment for most scrotal and other subcutaneous abscesses, but empirical antibiotic treatment is frequently administered in tandem, or, in rare cases, in isolation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r60">[60]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r61">[61]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r62">[62]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r63">[63]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r64">[64]</xref>&#x000a0;Antibiotics alone, along with warm compresses or hot sitz baths, can sometimes be used as definitive management for very small collections without overlying cutaneous&#x000a0;infection&#x000a0;or systemic features, and for patients who are not suitable candidates for surgical management.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r60">[60]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r64">[64]</xref>&#x000a0;Sitz baths may also be used postoperatively to promote wound cleansing.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>The most recent guidelines from the Infectious Diseases Society of America, updated in 2014, recommend adjunctive antibiotics&#x000a0;targeting&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic>&#x000a0;when&#x000a0;systemic features of infection are present.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r60">[60]</xref>&#x000a0;Targeted antibiotics based on cultures and sensitivity results should be used for recurrent abscesses.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r60">[60]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Results from a systematic review and meta-analysis of 14 randomized controlled trials involving 4198 patients found that antibiotics significantly reduce the risk of treatment failure, hospitalization, and recurrence; however, their use is associated with increased gastrointestinal adverse effects.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r65">[65]</xref>&#x000a0;Findings from another meta-analysis of 4 studies involving 2406 patients supported this, highlighting a higher rate of clinical cure with antibiotics compared with placebo.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r66">[66]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Culture results are frequently unobtainable, infrequently alter clinical management or initial antibiotic selection, and may be unnecessary without demonstrable impact on patient outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r67">[67]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r68">[68]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r69">[69]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r70">[70]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r71">[71]</xref>&#x000a0;Although this may be true in simple, uncomplicated cases with small (&#x0003c;2 cm) lesions in otherwise healthy adults, a study found that culture results directly led to changes in therapy in&#x000a0;65% of patients, particularly in those with high-risk factors.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r27">[27]</xref></p>
        <p>A wound culture is still recommended because the results may&#x000a0;help guide antibiotic selection in complex or recalcitrant cases with prolonged cellulitis, in patients&#x000a0;with diabetes mellitus or immunocompromise,&#x000a0;in&#x000a0;patients at the extremes of age, or when systemic symptoms are present. Cultures are also advisable for recurrent and large abscesses (&#x0003e;5 cm), infections from human or animal bites, antibiotic resistance, and epidemiological tracking.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r60">[60]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r72">[72]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r73">[73]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r74">[74]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r75">[75]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r76">[76]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r77">[77]</xref>&#x000a0;Overall, antibiotics appear beneficial for reducing recurrent abscess formation and improving outcomes,&#x000a0;regardless of abscess size, causative organism, comorbidities, or systemic signs.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r62">[62]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r63">[63]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r65">[65]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r66">[66]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r78">[78]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r79">[79]</xref></p>
        <p>Aspiration of purulent material for culture before incision of the abscess is superior to a specimen obtained after the abscess is opened due to a lower risk of bacterial contamination.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r77">[77]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r80">[80]</xref>&#x000a0;The overlying skin of the abscess should first be properly disinfected, and a large-bore needle should be used for aspiration. A Gram stain is also recommended because it provides more rapid information about the infecting organism than culture, which requires 24 to 48 hours to yield results.</p>
        <p>Antibiotic use and selection should be considered on an individual basis, taking into account each patient's clinical condition, site and severity of the infection, presence of systemic disease, comorbidities, drug excretion and metabolism, renal and liver function, mode of administration, adverse effects, local antibiotic resistance patterns, medication allergies, most likely causitive pathogens, likelihood of progression, and risk of complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r81">[81]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r82">[82]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r83">[83]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r84">[84]</xref></p>
        <p>Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) testing for methicillin-resistant <italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic>&#x000a0;(MRSA) can provide a rapid (&#x0003c;2 hours) and reliable indication of MRSA presence in the abscess.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r85">[85]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r86">[86]</xref>&#x000a0;With an outstanding negative predictive value of &#x0003e;98%, the test can reliably rule out MRSA, thereby avoiding the use of inappropriate and unnecessary anti-MRSA antibiotics.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r87">[87]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r88">[88]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r89">[89]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r90">[90]</xref><bold>&#x000a0;</bold>PCR testing detects bacterial DNA, which is present even in nonviable microorganisms; therefore, it can be used even after antibiotic administration.<bold><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r91">[91]</xref></bold>&#x000a0;However, PCR testing provides no information on antibiotic sensitivities and has a low positive predictive value.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r87">[87]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r89">[89]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r92">[92]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r93">[93]</xref>&#x000a0;Nevertheless, it can be very helpful in selecting initial antibiotic therapy and should be used where available.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Commonly Used Antibiotics for Scrotal Abscesses</bold>
</p>
        <p>Empiric antibiotic therapy for scrotal abscesses may include cefazolin, piperacillin-tazobactam, clindamycin, imipenem, vancomycin, trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, and tetracyclines, such as doxycycline and minocycline, which are often used in combination.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r94">[94]</xref><bold>&#x000a0;</bold>Mild infections may be treated with oral antibiotics, whereas more severe abscesses require parenteral therapy.</p>
        <p>Most strains of&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic>&#x000a0;are now methicillin-resistant, making penicillin derivatives ineffective. Effective antibiotics include oral doxycycline (100 mg twice daily),&#x000a0;minocycline (200 mg initial dose, then 100 mg daily), trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole (1 or 2 double-strength tablets twice daily), and intravenous (IV) tigecycline (100 mg IV initial dose, then 50 mg twice daily).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r95">[95]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r96">[96]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r97">[97]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r98">[98]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resources, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30613">Trimethoprim Sulfamethoxazole</ext-link>,"&#x000a0;"<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/38624">Doxycycline Hyclate</ext-link>," and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/25180">Minocycline</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
        <p>Parenteral vancomycin (15-20 mg/kg every 8-12 hours (preferred) or&#x000a0;1 g IV twice daily) is effective.&#x000a0;However, antistaphylococcal penicillins, particularly cefazolin (1 g IV every 6-8 hours), are safer, less nephrotoxic, and preferred if the organism is susceptible.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r95">[95]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r96">[96]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r97">[97]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r98">[98]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r99">[99]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r100">[100]</xref>&#x000a0;Please&#x000a0;see StatPearls' companion resource,&#x000a0;"<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30965">Vancomycin</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
        <p>Alternatives to vancomycin include daptomycin (6 mg/kg IV daily),&#x000a0;ceftaroline (600 mg IV twice daily),&#x000a0;and linezolid (600 mg IV or orally twice daily).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r101">[101]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r102">[102]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r103">[103]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resources, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30965">Vancomycin</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/32876">Daptomycin</ext-link>," and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/24291">Linezolid</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
        <p>Clindamycin (450 mg orally every 8 hours)&#x000a0;may be used; however, susceptibility varies geographically. If the isolate is erythromycin-resistant, clindamycin susceptibility should be confirmed with the D-test.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r104">[104]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r105">[105]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r106">[106]</xref>&#x000a0;The D-test is a disk diffusion method used to identify inducible clindamycin resistance in erythromycin-resistant staphylococci and streptococci. In these cases, erythromycin can act as a resistance-inducing agent.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r104">[104]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r105">[105]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r106">[106]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Antibiotics for mild infections (oral):</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Empiric:&#x000a0;Trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>General or gastrointestinal:&#x000a0;Cephalexin or amoxicillin/clavulanate</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>MRSA:&#x000a0;Add clindamycin or trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole (clindamycin carries an increased risk for <italic toggle="yes">Clostridioides difficile</italic> infection)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sexually transmitted infection (<italic toggle="yes">Chlamydia or Gonorrhea </italic>species): Ceftriaxone (intramuscular) + doxycycline, minocycline, or azithromycin</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Antibiotics for severe infections (parenteral):&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Broad-spectrum or hospital-acquired:&#x000a0;Piperacillin/tazobactam IV&#x000a0;&#x000b1; vancomycin IV (for MRSA) or imipenem/meropenem IV</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Fluoroquinolone:&#x000a0;Ciprofloxacin/levofloxacin + metronidazole (for anaerobic bacteria) &#x000b1; vancomycin IV.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Mixed infections (gastrointestinal and sexually transmitted disease):&#x000a0;Ceftriaxone (intramuscular) + doxycycline (orally twice daily) or azithromycin (oral).</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Indications for parenteral antibiotic therapy:&#x000a0;</bold>Indications are based on the severity and extent of the abscess, as well as patient factors and comorbidities. Hospital admission is typically required to ensure appropriate preoperative administration. These indications include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Evidence of immunocompromise (current or recent administration of cancer chemotherapy or immunosuppressive drugs, neutropenia, HIV, and frequent opportunistic infections)&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Inability to swallow or absorb oral antibiotics</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Large abscess or extensive surrounding cellulitis&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Rapid progression of infection</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Systemic signs of infection (fever &#x0003e;38 &#x000b0;C, hypotension, and tachycardia)</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Surgical Management</bold>
</p>
        <p>Incision and drainage is the standard recommended treatment for subcutaneous and scrotal abscesses.&#x000a0;Initial management of scrotal abscesses should ensure adequate pain relief and prevention of complications. In most cases, this includes the appropriate use of antibiotics based on clinical judgement, antibiograms, infection severity, patient factors, and local protocols.&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/23431">Incision and Drainage</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
        <p>Incision and drainage may&#x000a0;be performed under local or general anesthesia, with the choice of anesthesia typically influenced by the abscess depth and severity, clinical factors, surgical judgment, and patient preferences.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r107">[107]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r108">[108]</xref>&#x000a0;Advantages of local anesthesia include avoiding hospital admissions, fewer inpatient days, improved operating room efficiency, shorter delays in definitive treatment, faster return to normal activities, and reduced overall costs.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r61">[61]</xref>&#x000a0;However, local anesthesia is less effective in infected tissue due to the low pH, which reduces the percentage of the more active, uncharged form of the anesthetic.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r109">[109]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r110">[110]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/23431">Incision and Drainage</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
        <p>Large abscesses (&#x0003e;5 cm), anatomical abnormalities, closely adjacent vasculature, equivocal or atypical ultrasonography findings, or unusually complex lesions&#x000a0;are best treated surgically.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r111">[111]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r112">[112]</xref>&#x000a0;When performed under general anesthesia, appropriate preoperative preparation and coordination with the anesthesiologist and&#x000a0;interprofessional teams are essential to ensure patient safety and procedural efficiency.&#x000a0;In such cases, preoperative antibiotics should be administered.</p>
        <p>In an appropriately anesthetized patient, an&#x000a0;incision is made with a scalpel over the area of greatest fluctuance.&#x000a0;Purulent material is expressed, and curettage is performed to&#x000a0;remove loculations and necrotic, infected tissue.&#x000a0;Extensive irrigation with sterile saline is performed, followed by irrigation with an antiseptic solution, such as 0.05% chlorhexidine gluconate (preferred) or 1% betadine.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r113">[113]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r114">[114]</xref>&#x000a0;Hemostasis via diathermy may be required in some cases. Depending on the size of the abscess cavity, it may be packed with a hydrofiber dressing to maintain hemostasis. The wound is left open to heal by secondary intention, preventing premature skin closure before the infection is fully eradicated and avoiding an empty subcutaneous cavity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r113">[113]</xref></p>
        <p>An alternative surgical procedure to conventional incision and drainage for subcutaneous abscesses is loop drainage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r81">[81]</xref>&#x000a0;In this method, 2 small incisions are made at opposite poles of the abscess, loculations are removed, and a vessel loop is inserted through the incisions to allow continuous drainage of the cavity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r81">[81]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r115">[115]</xref>&#x000a0;Needle aspiration is a less invasive technique for abscess drainage, but it is generally not recommended due to its high failure rate.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r81">[81]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r116">[116]</xref>&#x000a0;However, needle aspiration is considered the optimal method for obtaining a pus sample for Gram staining and culture.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r77">[77]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r80">[80]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r116">[116]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-170151.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The differential diagnosis of scrotal abscess includes a broad range of conditions that may present with a scrotal swelling, tenderness, or erythema, including:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Epididymal cyst</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Epidermoid neoplasm</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Epididymo-orchitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Folliculitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Fournier gangrene</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Granuloma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hematoma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hydrocele</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Inguinal hernia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lymphoma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pyocele</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ruptured appendicitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Scrotal pustule</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Scrotal pyocele</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sebaceous cyst</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Spermatocele</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Testicular abscess</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Testicular torsion</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Testicular tumor&#x000a0;or neoplasm</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Torsed appendix testis (hydatid of Morgagni)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Varicocele</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-170151.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>With early diagnosis and appropriate management, scrotal abscesses have an excellent prognosis. In otherwise healthy individuals, most cases resolve without significant complications. However, delays to treatment or factors known to&#x000a0;impair wound healing, such as diabetes mellitus, immunosuppression, tobacco smoking, corticosteroid use, or obesity, may delay recovery time or increase the risk of progression to systemic infection or other&#x000a0;complications. The recurrence of scrotal abscesses should prompt evaluation for underlying predisposing conditions.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-170151.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Most scrotal abscesses resolve with appropriate surgical management. However, if left untreated or inadequately managed, they may progress to a range of local and systemic complications. Potential complications include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Cellulitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Extension into the deeper scrotal and perineal tissues</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Failure to resolve (refractory)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Fistula formation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Progression to Fournier gangrene, a life-threatening form of necrotizing fasciitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Prolonged healing time</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Recurrence</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Scarring</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sinus tract formation</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Timely and appropriate medical management is essential to reduce the risk of these complications. However, abscesses recur in approximately 10% of cases, even with incision and drainage and a complete course of antibiotics.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r62">[62]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r63">[63]</xref>&#x000a0;Reasons for this include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Bacterial resistance to the chosen antibiotic, especially for MRSA. When the culture and sensitivity results are available, the antibiotic choice should be optimized</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Incomplete antibiotic treatment duration</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Fistula formation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Inadequate abscess drainage or loculations (identified with ultrasonography&#x000a0;and repeat wound exploration)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Incorrect or inadequate antibiotic dosage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Misdiagnosis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Presence of a foreign body</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Undiagnosed comorbid conditions such as HIV, immunosuppression, and diabetes mellitus</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-170151.s12" sec-type="Postoperative and Rehabilitation Care">
        <title>Postoperative and Rehabilitation Care</title>
        <p>
<bold>Postoperative Care</bold>
</p>
        <p>Following incision and drainage of scrotal abscesses, postoperative care should focus on promoting wound healing, patient comfort, preventing recurrence, and monitoring for complications. As the wound is typically left open to heal by secondary intention, patients should be instructed in wound care and in monitoring for signs of complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r117">[117]</xref>&#x000a0;Depending on the type of packing and&#x000a0;the goals (hemostasis in the immediate postoperative period versus maintaining an open cavity to promote healing by secondary intention), appropriate nursing follow-up for wound review and packing changes should be considered.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r118">[118]</xref></p>
        <p>Analgesia should be optimized to minimize postoperative discomfort, particularly during dressing changes. Vacuum-assisted closure dressings should be considered.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r119">[119]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r120">[120]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r121">[121]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r122">[122]</xref>&#x000a0;Scrotal supports may reduce swelling and alleviate postoperative discomfort after various scrotal procedures. The utility of repeated packing in subcutaneous abscesses is controversial. Recent studies have questioned the benefits of repeated packing, suggesting it may lead to worse outcomes, increased pain, and prolonged&#x000a0;healing times.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r123">[123]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Vacuum-Assisted Wound Care</bold>
</p>
        <p>Vacuum-assisted wound care is effective for treating scrotal abscesses, particularly for large abscesses or after debridement for more severe infections.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r124">[124]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r125">[125]</xref>&#x000a0;Vacuum therapy for wounds promotes faster healing by removing excess exudate, preventing fluid accumulation, increasing blood flow, facilitating management of large defects, reducing hospitalization days, promoting granulation tissue growth, and minimizing the need for frequent dressing changes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r119">[119]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r120">[120]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r121">[121]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r122">[122]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r124">[124]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r125">[125]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r126">[126]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r127">[127]</xref>&#x000a0;However, achieving adequate positioning and air seal can be challenging in this anatomical region.&#x000a0;The use of skin adhesives, surgical gloves, vacuum-assisted closure dressings, and vacuum-assisted closure baskets can be beneficial.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r121">[121]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r128">[128]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r129">[129]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r130">[130]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r131">[131]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Hyperbaric Oxygen Therapy</bold>
</p>
        <p>Hyperbaric oxygen therapy may be considered postoperatively as an adjunctive treatment in more severe or recalcitrant infection with significant cellulitis. In early Fournier gangrene, hyperbaric oxygen therapy enhances the effects of antibiotics, reduces inflammation, increases blood flow, eliminates anaerobic bacteria,&#x000a0;and promotes healing.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r132">[132]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r133">[133]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-170151.r134">[134]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-170151.s13" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>Patients presenting with scrotal abscesses may be treated initially by primary care or emergency clinicians. Definitive management typically involves consultation with a urologist or, in some cases, a general surgeon to perform and&#x000a0;oversee the surgical treatment. Additional specialist involvement may include radiologists and ultrasonography technicians, who perform and interpret diagnostic imaging such as ultrasonography. Anesthesiologists&#x000a0;are involved in the care of patients who have incision and drainage performed under anesthesia. Infectious disease specialists may help with antibiotic selection. Plastic surgeons and wound care specialists may help with postoperative care. Nurses are integral to the management and aftercare of abscesses, particularly regarding wound care and cavity packing. Patients with hidradenitis suppurativa may benefit from a dermatological assessment, although severe disease may require consultation with plastic or general surgery.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-170151.s14" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Effective prevention of scrotal abscesses relies on managing modifiable risk factors and improving awareness of early symptoms. Patients should be educated and encouraged to maintain good personal hygiene, including regular bathing of the genital area to reduce the risk of bacterial colonization and skin breakdown. Men should use genital protection during sports. For patients with diabetes mellitus, improving glycemic control may reduce the risk of skin and soft-tissue infections and improve wound healing. Smoking cessation should be encouraged, as it may reduce the incidence of complications and improve wound healing.</p>
        <p>Key preventive measures include&#x000a0;keeping the scrotal area clean and dry; using condoms; staying up to date with all recommended vaccines, such as mumps; avoiding scrotal skin trauma from shaving or waxing; and maintaining good urinary Foley catheter care. Regular testicular self-examinations also help identify issues early.</p>
        <p>Warning patients to be vigilant for signs of worsening infection postoperatively is important to ensure early detection of complications and facilitate timely care. Education should include monitoring for increasing pain, erythema, swelling, discharge, and systemic&#x000a0;signs such as fever, rigors, or malaise. Clear guidance should be provided to patients on when and how to seek medical attention to reduce the risk of serious complications.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-170151.s15" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>The following are clinical pearls regarding scrotal abscesses:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Optimizing lifestyle factors and managing comorbidities can reduce the risk of a recurrence.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Early recognition and treatment are critical to avoid progression, especially in patients with predisposing risk factors.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>If cultures are obtained, results should be reviewed within 24 to 48 hours to guide therapy.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Packing of abscess cavities is controversial; routine repeated packing is not recommended for most abscesses. Tight packing may be appropriate initially for hemostasis, but when packing material is replaced, it should be packed loosely.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Vacuum-assisted closure dressings may be considered when appropriate and available.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>PCR testing for MRSA is highly reliable when negative, potentially avoiding unnecessary anti-MRSA antibiotic use.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Risk factors include diabetes mellitus,&#x000a0;immunosuppression, poor hygiene, smoking, advanced age, alcohol use disorder, obesity, atherosclerosis, malnutrition, and the use of SGLT2 inhibitors.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Scrotal abscesses need to be distinguished from deeper infections, such as epididymo-orchitis, pyoceles, or testicular abscesses, by careful clinical assessment and, if necessary, ultrasonography.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole double-strength (1-2 tablets twice daily) is a good empiric antibiotic choice for oral therapy if culture reports are unavailable.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-170151.s16" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Effective management of scrotal abscesses relies on a coordinated, patient-centric, interprofessional approach to minimize pain and distress and optimize outcomes. Early diagnosis,&#x000a0;the use&#x000a0;of imaging when indicated,&#x000a0;prompt intervention, and effective postoperative care require collaboration between healthcare professionals across multiple disciplines. Primary and emergency care clinicians, general surgeons, and urologists play pivotal roles in clinical assessment and surgical treatment.</p>
        <p>When surgical treatment is provided, anesthesiologists and nursing staff provide essential perioperative support. Involving a radiologist or an ultrasonography technician can provide vital imaging to aid diagnosis. Nurses are integral to the healthcare team, particularly during the postoperative phase, when they are often the primary clinicians responsible for wound care, pain management, patient education, and monitoring for signs of complications. Wound care specialists may assist in postoperative treatment if healing is delayed for unexplained reasons.</p>
        <p>A discussion with the patient about the options of both operative and nonoperative treatment is essential to ensure informed consent before any procedure and to uphold patient autonomy throughout their healthcare journey. Shared decision-making empowers patients to actively engage with their care. Integrating knowledge and skills from a broad range of healthcare professionals, combined with effective communication and shared decision-making with the patient, ensures that care is optimized and aligns with the patient's preferences, values, and clinical needs. A strategic approach is equally crucial, involving evidence-based strategies to optimize treatment plans and minimize&#x000a0;adverse effects. Ethical considerations must guide decision-making, ensuring informed consent and respecting patient autonomy in treatment choices.</p>
        <p>Each healthcare professional must be aware of their responsibilities and contribute their unique expertise to the patient's care plan, fostering an interdisciplinary approach. Effective interprofessional communication is paramount, allowing seamless information exchange and collaborative decision-making among the team members. Care coordination plays a pivotal role in ensuring that the patient's journey from diagnosis to treatment and follow-up is well managed, minimizing errors and enhancing patient safety. By embracing these principles of skill, strategy, ethics, responsibility, interprofessional communication, and care coordination, healthcare professionals can deliver patient-centered care, ultimately improving patient outcomes and enhancing team performance in the management of scrotal abscess.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-170151.s17">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
